WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.130
Steve Irwin

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:03.770
He was a force of nature - the quintessential
Aussie outdoorsmen.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:08.420
With his wicked sense of humor and his insatiable
passion for wildlife, Steve Irwin - the crocodile

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.140
hunter - became a worldwide celebrity.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:16.400
He shone a spotlight on man’s mismanagement
of our natural resources and championed the

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.640
plight of the planet’s most vulnerable inhabitants
- the animals.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:24.130
But Steve was also a daredevil - constantly
pushing the limits in his interactions with

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.130
the wild.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:28.800
Then, in a tragic moment, he was gone, taken
from us far too soon.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.700
In this Biographics we get to grips with the
Crocodile Hunter from Down Under, Steve Irwin.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:49.310
The Wilderness Child

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:53.480
Stephen Robert Irwin was born on February
22nd, 1962 in Melbourne, Australia.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.890
Both of his parents were passionate about
wildlife.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.470
His father, Bob, was a plumber, but he was
also a keen amatuer herpetologist who studied

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.579
amphibians, while mother Lyn was a wildlife
rehabilitator.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:06.950
When Steve was just a few years old the family
moved to Queensland, where Bob and and Lyn

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.960
opened the Queensland Reptile and Fauna Park.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.530
Steve, therefore, grew up around animals and
was soon put to work at the park.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.840
He would clean out the cages and feed the
crocodiles and lizards.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.270
By the age of six, he was completely accustomed
to being around creatures that most children

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:21.649
would be terrified of.

00:01:21.649 --> 00:01:26.210
His whole existence was surrounded by wildlife,
from crocodiles to kangaroos and koalas.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.929
The Irwin’s developed their Reptile park
from nothing.

00:01:28.929 --> 00:01:32.499
They spent the first couple of years living
in a small caravan as they slowly added to

00:01:32.499 --> 00:01:33.819
their animal inventory.

00:01:33.819 --> 00:01:37.229
The first snakes they put on display were
ones that Bob and Steve captured while out

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:38.229
fishing.

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:41.990
Meanwhile Lyn was busy rehabilitating orphaned
joeys, kangaroos, koalas, wombats, snakes

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:43.110
and crays.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.979
At the same time, she was raising three children
of her own.

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:50.029
Steve proved to be a mischievous child, who
was always testing the patience of his father.

00:01:50.029 --> 00:01:54.279
He would constantly go missing, ony to be
discovered down at the local creek chasing

00:01:54.279 --> 00:01:55.279
lizards.

00:01:55.279 --> 00:01:56.780
Yet, he wasn’t being rebellious.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.219
He wanted to find venomous snakes and lizards
so that he could show them to his dad and

00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:02.389
win his approval.

00:02:02.389 --> 00:02:06.259
But rather than making his father proud, Steve’s
constant wanderings left Bob frustrated.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:09.229
They boy was hyperactive, never sitting still.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.090
From the age of two, he developed a love of,
and skill for, climbing trees.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.670
So, the first thing that his family members
would do when he went missing was to look

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:16.670
up.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.210
On the way to school, if he saw a dead reptile
lying on the road, Steve would make his mother

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.290
stop so that he could get out and investigate.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:26.770
When he was six years old, he got the best
present he could have wished for - a twelve

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.420
foot long python.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.770
Steve absolutely adored his new best friend.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.640
At school, Steve was considered a bit of an
oddity by his peers.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.220
He wasn’t into the normal boy things, like
bicycles, sport and skateboards.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.320
He spent his lunchtimes wandering around the
school yard looking for birds, lizards and

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.480
any other creatures he could find.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.250
After a while, other kids got involved, looking
to Steve as the local expert on all things

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.370
related to wildlife.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.980
Bob Irwin was a hero and a mentor to his son.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.420
He was a wildlife innovator who was at the
cutting edge of animal husbandry.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.270
With the Wildlife Park established with a
good supply of smaller reptiles and animals,

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.400
Bob decided it was time to add a couple of
crocodiles.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.890
He took nine year old Steve with him on his
first crocodile hunt.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:12.060
As expected, the boy was extremely active
and it was all his dad could do to keep him

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.060
in the boat.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.230
When a croc was sighted, Steve would want
to be the first one to grab it.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:20.250
He was positioned at the front of the boat
with the spotlight while his father was operating

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.590
the outboard motor.

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:25.850
As they got close, Steve fixed his gaze on
the eyes of what he thought was a small croc.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:30.340
The he pounced out of the boat and into the
water, grabbing it by the neck.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.100
But the boy hadn’t realized how large this
crocodile was.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.130
It was, in fact about the same size as him
- and quite a bit stronger.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.130
The boy and the croc struggled for a while.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.590
When it became clear to Bob that his son was
getting the worst of the encounter, he reached

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:44.650
his arm into the water and scooped up both
of them, scooping the boy and the croc into

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.330
the boat and pinning the animal down with
his body.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:51.650
Recovering quickly, Steve took over the job
of holding down the croc, a huge smile on

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:57.470
his face - it was the proudest moment of his
life so far.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:05.530
The Crocodile Hunter

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.819
In the early ‘70’s reports of crocodiles
attacking people were becoming more frequent

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:11.150
around Australia.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.380
As the stock of crocodiles at the Reptile
Park grew, Bob decided to involve himself

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:19.190
in attempts to resolve the conflicts between
crocodiles and humans.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:23.810
He joined the East Coast Crocodile Management
Program and he and Steve threw themselves

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:28.190
into the task of capturing and relocating
crocs from areas where there was potential

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:30.150
for a nasty encounter with humans.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:35.160
It was extremely dangerous and exciting work
and Steve excelled at it.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.690
He had a natural gift for, not only locating
the crocs, but also for capturing them.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:44.710
He would get absolutely focused on catching
a crocodile, as if he were entering into one-on-one

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:45.760
combat with it.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.130
Once the croc was secured and subdued in his
net, he would get a huge adrenaline rush,

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.250
which would make him keen for the next encounter.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:56.080
By the time he was in his late teens, Steve
was living in the bush for extended periods

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:01.200
of time, camping out under the stars and spending
days at a time in pursuit of a single croc.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.940
He had to live off the land and the water,
honing his bush skills while developing the

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.950
mystique of the crocodile hunter.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.930
It was gruelling, physically demanding work
- and most of the time Steve was on his own.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:14.600
He was young, fit, keen and innovative.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.600
Steve became the most successful crocodile
hunter in the land.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.380
With his growing reputation came a degree
of complacency.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.470
He began to feel that he could do anything.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:26.041
On one occasion he got a croc back to his
camp, ready to put into a crate but he had

00:05:26.041 --> 00:05:28.170
neglected to secure the animal’s mouth.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:32.230
Not realizing this, Steve let the croc out
of its net as he prepared the crate.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:37.110
Then, with his back turned, the croc attacked,
snapping its huge jaws around his leg.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:41.419
Fortunately, Steve got out of the situation
with nothing but a few holes in his foot.

00:05:41.419 --> 00:05:45.669
On another occasion, Bob went up into northern
Queensland in search of an infamous croc known

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:46.940
as ‘Aggro.’

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:50.490
This creature would pop it’s head out of
the water right next to boats, horrifying

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:51.490
the locals.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:55.660
Bob’s goal was to locate the beast and set
up a marker so that his son could follow up

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:57.139
and execute the capture.

00:05:57.139 --> 00:06:01.680
Bob located the croc easily and made a mark
in an overhanging tree so that Steve would

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.080
know where to set the trap.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.460
Yet, from the moment that he went in, Steve
could feel that ‘Aggro’ was stalking him.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:11.830
As he was setting the trap something bumped
into his boat and his dog began barking.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:14.100
Steve quickly set the trap and then left.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:17.260
Now it was a matter of watching and waiting.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.940
On the third day, Steve headed for the site
only to discover that the trap was completely

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.470
submerged under water.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:24.919
The bait had been taken and the trap had gone
off.

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:29.380
Steve brought his boat close and grabbed hold
of the net, ready to drag the captured croc

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:30.380
on deck.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.150
Suddenly there was a massive upheaval as the
croc smashed into the boat from under the

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:35.150
water.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:39.270
A stunned Steve managed to maintain his grip
on the net, but then another blow rocked the

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:40.449
boat, and then another.

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:44.139
But Steve held on and began dragging the net
in.

00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:46.550
Finally he managed to get the tail on board.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:51.290
But then the entire body of the croc catapulted
out of the water and into the air coming down

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:53.020
beside Steve in the boat.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:56.220
The man and his dog immediately jumped into
the water.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:00.980
Steve now grabbed whatever he could - branches,
ropes, mud and threw it on top of the violently

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:02.280
thrashing crocodile.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.830
The ideal thing would have been to blindfold
the animal, but that was out of the question.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:09.010
Steve then jumped into the boat and onto the
back of the croc.

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:11.830
He threw more netting over the beast, trying
to cover its eyes.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:14.620
Then he fired up the outboard motor and started
for shore.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:19.550
All the way back the crocodile fought so violently
that Steve was convinced the boat was about

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.120
to split apart.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.720
Steve finally got the one ton crocodile secured
and restrained at his camp.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.410
His next problem was how to move it.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:30.680
He decided to go back to a local farm and
recruit some helpers.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.560
But when the cane farmers saw the size of
‘Aggro’ they refused to get out of their

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:35.560
boat.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.660
Steve had to resort to getting a front end
loader to shift the croc.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:42.270
It was experiences like these that turned
Steve into a local legend.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.919
Yet the man himself had mixed feelings.

00:07:43.919 --> 00:07:54.340
He felt regret that he had had to remove Aggro,
the king of that land, from his natural environment.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:57.550
Australia Zoo

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:00.470
By 1990 Steve had recaptured more than a hundred
crocs.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:04.270
Some of them were kept at the Reptile Park
while the majority were released into the

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:05.270
wild.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:09.229
In 1991, Bob and Lyn decided that Steve had
come of age and was ready to take full control

00:08:09.229 --> 00:08:10.229
of the park.

00:08:10.229 --> 00:08:14.380
Steve’s goal was to make his parents proud
and he knew he would do it by turning the

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:18.720
park into the greatest wildlife facilities
on the face of the earth.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.100
His first step was to rename the park ‘Australia
Zoo.’ he then threw himself into creating

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.910
the most exciting, educational and entertaining
wildlife experience that zoo visitors would

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:29.080
ever experience.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:32.860
The highlight was the crocodile show where
Steve would be right up close and personal

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.229
with crocs, totally in control and showing
no fear.

00:08:36.229 --> 00:08:39.450
He would tell the crowds what passionate and
loving animals they were.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:44.150
With his huge personality and boundless energy
he quickly won over the public and attendance

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:48.740
figures went through the roof.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.649
Terri

00:08:51.649 --> 00:09:01.950
In 1991, among the tens of thousands of visitors
to Australia Zoo was Terri Raines, a veterinarian

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:04.060
and animal lover from the United States.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:08.880
She was looking for a place to relocate cougars
that had been used in the entertainment industry.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.370
She was drawn to the crocodile demonstration
and the fast talking Aussie who was in with

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:14.820
the deadly creatures.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.589
Terri wrote down her immediate impressions
of Steve . . .

00:09:17.589 --> 00:09:20.090
I thought there was no-one like this anywhere
in the world.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:25.140
He sounded like an environmental Tarzan, a
larger-than life superhero guy.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:29.399
Steve managed to catch sight of Terri in the
crowd and, according to him it was love at

00:09:29.399 --> 00:09:30.399
first sight.

00:09:30.399 --> 00:09:33.720
The two kindred spirits were engaged four
months after that first encounter.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:38.100
They married in Eugene, Oregon, Terri’s
home town, on June 4th, 1992.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.570
Their honeymoon was spent hunting crocodiles
in Northern Queensland.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:45.380
To say that Terri was out of her element on
this trip was an understatement.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:49.400
She had never been into the harsh Australian
outback and she had definitely never been

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:50.890
on a croc hunt before.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:51.890
But.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:55.430
trusting implicitly in the man that she referred
to as her ‘prince’, she threw herself

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:56.790
into the experience.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:10:01.410
Accompanying the Irwin’s on their honeymoon
adventure was Steve’s friend, John Stainton.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:05.450
Stainton filmed the action of the croc hunt
and other adventures, including Terri’s

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:08.520
first encounter with a six foot long black
snake.

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:12.510
This footage became the first episode of a
new Animal Planet TV show called The Crocodile

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:13.510
Hunter.

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:17.720
The series which featured both Steve and Terri
was so successful that a second, and then

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:19.580
a third, series were ordered.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:25.540
It was a hit in over 130 countries, with hundreds
of millions of people embracing Steve’s

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:28.120
lovable, over the top Aussie personality.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:30.220
His trademark catchphrase, ‘Crikey!’

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:34.440
became known the world over.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:38.600
The final episode of series three was entitled
‘Steve’s Last Adventure’.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:42.600
It followed him across the world as he embarked
upon adventures everywhere from the Himalayas,

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.390
to South Africa and China.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:49.040
The Crocodile Hunter series allowed Steve
to take his conservation message to the world.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:53.790
He viewed it as his life’s mission to work
tirelessly to save the world’s endangered

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:54.790
species.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:59.100
As a result of his name recognition, he became
the front man for a number of media campaigns

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.830
and became a passionate ambassador for Australian
tourism.

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:07.260
Steve crusaded for wildlife preservation,
using his fame to bring the issue to public

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:08.260
consciousness.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:12.920
In the late 90’s he also discovered a new
species of sea turtle and was at the forefront

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:14.830
of a campaign against animal poaching.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:19.750
He also funded large nature reserves not just
in Australia, but also in Fiji, Vanuatu and

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:20.750
the United States.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:24.580
Irwin’s TV series fame led to more television
and even some movie work.

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:28.839
He appeared in 2001’s Dr. Dolittle 2 as
himself and then starred in The Crocodile

00:11:28.839 --> 00:11:31.529
Hunter; Collision Course the following year.

00:11:31.529 --> 00:11:35.950
In 2006, he provided the voice for an elephant
seal named Trev in the movie Happy Feet.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.740
The money that Steve made from his TV and
movie appearances was poured into building

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:43.680
new exhibits for Australia Zoo and for funding
conservation projects.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:47.870
The zoo was designed as an environmental park,
with the comfort of the animals the foremost

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:48.870
concern.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:52.190
Fences and cages would only be used when absolutely
necessary.

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:56.400
The staff at the zoo grew to about 50 and
the area expanded to eventually take up 100

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:57.400
acres.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.880
The Zoo won the award for Best Australian
tourist attraction in 2003-4.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:05.360
In 2004 the Australian Animal Hospital was
opened right next to the zoo.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:09.180
The hospital cares for around 6,000 animals
each year.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:15.130
In 2006, Australia Zoo Retail won the Australian
Tourism Retailing Award.

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:23.180
A Family Man

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:28.420
Steve and Terri had two children together,
Bindi, born in 1998, and Robert, in 2003.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:32.310
Bindi’s birth was filmed and shown on TV
for the Crocodile Hunter series.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:35.750
Steve had been filming for the show when he
got the call that Terri’s waters had broken.

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:40.130
He turned up at the hospital two hours later
with a complete film crew in tow.

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:43.350
Terri was surprised but, as was her nature,
took it all in her stride.

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:46.519
They were both surprised, but delighted, that
the baby was a girl.

00:12:46.519 --> 00:12:50.870
Steve knew instantly what the name was going
to be - Bindi Sue, after his favorite croc

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:52.320
and his favorite dog.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:55.440
Steve was the self proclaimed proudest father
on the face of the earth.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:58.730
He and Bindi bonded instantly, becoming virtually
inseparable.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:02.500
She went on her first wildlife documentary
shoot at just six days old.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:06.920
Bindi would grow up in the international spotlight,
starring in her own show, Bindi: Jungle Girl

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:07.920
when she was eight years old.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:12.170
Although the first series aired the year after
Steve’s passing, it was shot before he died

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:16.230
and so he featured in several episodes.

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:24.560
Criticism and Praise

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:30.060
November of 2003 found Steve filming a documentary
off the coast of Baja California Peninsula

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:32.120
in Mexico about sea lions.

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:35.550
A report came through the boat’s radio that
a pair of scuba divers were missing in the

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:36.550
area.

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:38.951
Steve immediately shut down filming and he
and his team joined the search.

00:13:38.951 --> 00:13:43.260
The next day, members of his crew found one
of the divers clinging to a rocky cliff edge.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.959
Steve and another rescuer brought the man
back to their boat.

00:13:45.959 --> 00:13:48.779
The other man’s body was located a short
time later.

00:13:48.779 --> 00:13:53.010
In early 2004, Steve made the news for the
wrong reasons when video was shown of him

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:57.100
feeding a chicken carcass to a twelve and
a half foot saltwater crocodile while holding

00:13:57.100 --> 00:13:59.150
his one month old son, Robert.

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:03.110
Child welfare and animals rights groups alike
condemned the action, with some referring

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:04.190
to it as child abuse.

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:08.820
Both Steve and Terri contended that he had
full control over the situation, Nevertheless,

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:12.070
Steve did apologize, doing so on American
channel NBC.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:16.880
The incident prompted the Queensland Government
to enact a law stating that no untrained persons

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:19.800
or children were allowed inside a crocodile
enclosure.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.670
Steve was also criticized for his conservation
views, which some viewed as overly simplistic

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:26.550
and weighted towards tourism.

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:30.800
The Sydney Morning Herald wrote that his message
boiled down to ‘eating roos and crocs is

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:34.630
bad for tourism, and therefore more cruel
than eating other animals.’

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:39.030
There were, however, other industry experts
were poured praise upon Irwin and the work

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:42.430
that he had done to raise the public's awareness
of conservation issues.

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:47.380
Mark Townsend, the CEO of Queensland's RSPCA
called him a ‘modern day Noah,’ while

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:50.899
a Canadian environmentalist remarked after
Steves’ passing.

00:14:50.899 --> 00:14:53.440
. .
"humanity will not protect that which we fear

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.170
or do not understand.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:59.630
Steve Irwin helped us understand those things
that many people thought were a nuisance at

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:01.350
best, a horror at worst.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:04.470
That made him a great educator and conservationist.”

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:09.300
Steve’s own conservation mentor was British
conservationist and film-maker Sir David Attenborough.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:13.500
A month after Steve’s death, Terri Irwin
presented Attenborough with a Lifetime Achievement

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:14.500
Award.

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:18.350
In his acceptance speech, Sir David praised
Steve, saying that he “taught [people] how

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:22.279
wonderful and exciting nature was, and he
was a born communicator.”

00:15:22.279 --> 00:15:32.839
Death &amp; Legacy

00:15:32.839 --> 00:15:38.170
Throughout his 44 years, Steve had taken on
and defeated many of the most dangerous creatures

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:39.170
on the planet.

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:43.529
It was ironic then, that the animal that would
get the better of him was a normally tranquil

00:15:43.529 --> 00:15:44.529
fish.

00:15:44.529 --> 00:15:49.040
On September 4th, 2006, he was filming off
Australia’s Great Barrier Reef for a documentary

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:50.930
to be called Ocean’s Deadliest.

00:15:50.930 --> 00:15:54.620
The weather had been bad for three days, so
Steve had been sitting around with little

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:56.380
to do but wait for the weather to improve.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:16:00.320
Today he decided to a do a bit of snorkelling
in the shallow waters which could be filmed

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:02.110
for his daughter Bindi’s new TV show.

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:05.080
He got down into the water, which was only
at chest height.

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:09.290
Seeing a large blue Bull Stingray, he came
over top of it in order to film it as it swam

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:10.290
away.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:14.160
However, just as he hovered above it, the
stingray thrust up its tail and began stabbing

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:15.160
it upward.

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:17.779
A barb was shot directly into Steve’s chest.

00:16:17.779 --> 00:16:21.930
He immediately pulled it out and rose to the
top and struggled to get back onto the boat.

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:25.210
The crew administered CPR and then rushed
him to the nearest hospital.

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:27.180
He was announced dead on arrival.

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:31.290
Steve Irwin’s legacy lives on through his
wife Terri and his children, Bindi and Robert.

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:36.089
These three Wildlife Warriors continue to
run Australia Zoo and to honor the memory

00:16:36.089 --> 00:17:10.230
of this truly amazing man.

